# HTML Email Development Rules

When creating or modifying HTML emails, follow email-client compatibility best practices.

## General principles

- Treat HTML email as a compatibility-first environment.
- The email must work across Gmail, Outlook, Apple Mail, Yahoo Mail and common mobile email clients.
- Do not assume modern browser CSS support.
- Prefer proven, widely supported HTML/CSS techniques over modern web development patterns.
- Keep the markup simple and robust.

## HTML structure

- Prefer `<table>`-based layouts for the main email structure.
- Use `role="presentation"` on layout tables.
- Avoid relying on `<div>` elements for complex layout.
- Always include appropriate `cellpadding`, `cellspacing` and `border` attributes where necessary for compatibility.
- Use inline styles for critical styling.
- Do not use JavaScript.
- Do not use external CSS files.
- Avoid SVG when possible because support varies between email clients.
- Avoid forms, iframes and other interactive browser-oriented elements.

## CSS

- Prefer inline CSS for styles that affect layout and appearance.
- Do not rely on CSS variables.
- Avoid CSS Grid.
- Avoid `position: absolute` and `position: fixed`.
- Avoid relying on `float`.
- Avoid advanced selectors.
- Avoid `:hover` for essential functionality.
- Avoid CSS animations and transitions.
- Avoid `backdrop-filter`, `filter` and other modern CSS features unless there is a strong compatibility reason.
- Use explicit widths, padding, font sizes and line heights where appropriate.
- Use `font-family` fallback stacks.
- Use `line-height` explicitly for important text.
- Avoid shorthand CSS when compatibility may be affected.

## Responsive design

- Design mobile-first where practical.
- Use media queries for responsive behavior, but never make the email unusable if media queries are unsupported.
- The desktop version must remain functional without responsive CSS.
- Avoid layouts that depend entirely on flexbox or grid.
- Use fluid widths such as `width: 100%` together with a reasonable `max-width` where supported.
- Keep important content readable on small screens.

## Images

- Always provide meaningful `alt` text.
- Define image dimensions when possible.
- Use `display: block` on images to avoid unwanted gaps.
- Do not rely on background images for essential content.
- Use absolute HTTPS URLs for images in production emails.
- Do not use local filesystem paths or relative URLs for production email images.
- Consider that some clients block images by default.
- The email must remain understandable when images are disabled.

## Links and buttons

- Use real `<a>` elements for links.
- For buttons, prefer bulletproof email buttons using an `<a>` element styled appropriately.
- Do not rely on JavaScript click handlers.
- Make clickable areas sufficiently large for mobile users.
- Use absolute HTTPS URLs in production emails.
- Do not rely on `mailto:` or other protocols for essential functionality.

## Typography

- Prefer system/web-safe fonts with reliable fallbacks.
- Do not depend on custom web fonts for important content.
- Maintain sufficient contrast between text and background.
- Use explicit font sizes and line heights.
- Do not use extremely small text.

## Outlook compatibility

- Remember that desktop Outlook may use Microsoft Word's rendering engine.
- Avoid CSS features known to have poor Outlook support.
- Be cautious with margins, padding, background images, border radius and modern layout techniques.
- Prefer table cells and padding for reliable spacing.
- When a visual feature is important, provide an Outlook-compatible fallback.

## Accessibility

- Use semantic text hierarchy where practical.
- Provide meaningful `alt` attributes for informative images.
- Decorative images should have empty alt text when appropriate.
- Maintain sufficient color contrast.
- Do not communicate important information through color alone.
- Links must have descriptive text.

## Rails / ERB

- Keep application logic out of the presentation whenever possible.
- Preserve Rails mailer conventions.
- Use Rails URL helpers for links when appropriate.
- Production URLs must be absolute.
- Use the application's configured default URL options.
- Ensure asset/image URLs generated by Rails are absolute HTTPS URLs.
- Never reference development-only URLs in production emails.
- Escape user-provided content appropriately.
- Keep reusable email components/partials compatible with email-client limitations.

## CSS inlining

- If the project uses a CSS inliner, write maintainable source CSS first and let the inliner generate inline styles.
- Do not manually duplicate large amounts of inline CSS if an established inlining pipeline already exists.
- After inlining, verify that the generated HTML remains valid and compatible.

## Email safety checklist

Before considering an email complete, verify:

1. It does not depend on JavaScript.
2. It does not depend on external CSS.
3. Important styles are inline or will be reliably inlined.
4. The layout does not depend on CSS Grid.
5. The layout does not depend exclusively on Flexbox.
6. Images have absolute HTTPS URLs.
7. Images have appropriate alt text.
8. Links use absolute HTTPS URLs.
9. The email remains usable when images are blocked.
10. The email remains usable on mobile.
11. The email has reasonable fallbacks for Outlook.
12. No important information is hidden behind unsupported CSS.
13. Rails-generated URLs are production-safe.
14. User-provided content is properly escaped.
